    "message": "fix(analytics): search reasoning and tool calls, not just prompt and reply\n\nContent search matched only input_text and output_text. On a real log\nthat is a minority of what is stored: 55% of requests carry thinking\ntext and 78% carry tool calls, and neither was searchable. Verified\nagainst 600 real requests -- a phrase occurring only in reasoning and\none occurring only in a tool call each returned zero rows, while a\nphrase from the prompt returned every row.\n\nSearch now covers the prompt, the reply, the model's reasoning, and its\ntool calls. Tool calls match on their string values rather than their\nJSON encoding, because the encoding both misses (C:\\Users is stored\nescaped) and misleads (every row contains the key name \"command\").\n\nMulti-word queries now require every word to appear somewhere in the\nrequest rather than as an adjacent phrase, so \"proxy 8082\" finds a\nrequest whose prompt mentions the proxy and whose reasoning mentions the\nport. A single-word query is unchanged, and since a phrase match implies\nevery word is present this only ever adds results.\n\nCase folding moved from text to bytes, which is 7x cheaper on a 43 KB\nbody and matches SQLite's own LIKE -- ASCII-only. Folding in Python text\nwould have made compressed rows match things the inline rows beside them\ndid not. Searching two extra fields still costs less than before: a\nthree-word search over 600 real rows went 991ms -> 518ms against the\nfirst working version.\n\nSame assertions run against both storage forms via a parametrised\nfixture, so inline and compressed cannot drift apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
